@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@
 Build with Node 24 and npm from a clean checkout on Windows or macOS:
 
 ```sh
-node hosting/build.cjs PUBLIC_BUILD_SETTINGS_JSON OUTPUT_DIRECTORY
+node hosting/build.cjs PUBLIC_BUILD_SETTINGS_JSON OUTPUT_DIRECTORY [ABSOLUTE_CACHE_DIRECTORY]
 ```
 
 The output directory must not exist and must be outside the checkout. The build settings must be a JSON object containing only `NEXT_PUBLIC_` keys and string, finite number, or boolean values. Use `{}` if none are required. These values are public and can be embedded in browser assets; never supply production secrets.
@@ -19,3 +19,11 @@ SVIS packaging runs its host, proxy-header and PostgreSQL-adapter tests. OPS pac
 Run the isolated helper checks with `node --test hosting/helpers.node-test.cjs`. These validate the settings guard, subprocess environments, package layout, npm discovery and launcher without installing dependencies or starting applications.
 
 Deployment is not enabled by adding these files. The Windows controller, service configuration, initial baseline revision, and rollback checks must be installed and validated separately.
+
+## Deployment performance
+
+The optional cache directory is a persistent, build-account-owned directory outside the checkout and release. It stores npm downloads and, on non-Windows hosts for Next.js applications, compiler cache entries isolated by application, operating system, architecture, Node version, lockfile, Next configuration and public build settings. Do not point it at runtime configuration or production application directories. The Windows worker serializes builds; do not share this directory with another concurrent worker.
+
+Every revision still runs npm ci, the existing regression tests, and the complete production build. Windows compiles fresh: cross-checkout Turbopack cache restoration triggered runaway workers during validation and is disabled. On other platforms only .next/cache is reused; compiled releases and node_modules are not restored. Keep this cache outside build-retention directories. The helper emits per-phase timings and writes OUTPUT_DIRECTORY.timings.json without configuration values or credentials.
+
+SVIS can use hosting/runtime/package-lock.json for Windows server dependencies, excluding Expo/mobile dependencies from the server package. Its host bundle verifies every external package against this runtime manifest. Native Vercel/mobile builds retain the original application dependencies.
